when talking or thinking about crypto  it's important to get the nomenclature  right  there are three terms of art that are  extraordinarily important to master and  to understand the distinction between  them  first there's the notion of  cryptocurrency  second there's the notion of a token or  a coin  and third there's the notion of an  initial token offering or an initial  coin offering  all are different  all have different legal implications  and let's burrow down and let's  understand the distinction among the  three a cryptocurrency  is a form of code that's supposed to  represent some kind of value  it's an electronic version of money or  at least that's how some people explain  it other people will dispute that  explanation  best example is probably something like  bitcoin where many people are using it  as a store of value on relatively rare  occasion it's actually used as a medium  of exchange but basically it's supposed  to represent value in a sense like a  dollar bill or you know some kind of  asset or gold or what have you  but in the form of a code that is scarce  that doesn't exist in an infinite form  we then have another form of crypto that  many people call coins or tokens  these are codes  that when used in a particular system  will perform a particular function  for example  you could imagine an environment in  which you can store your data  on the internet on other people's  computers  it's a little bit like airbnb but for  your data instead of for your body all  right  and when you've got that kind of a  system you're going to have to pay the  people who are providing the servers  where your data resides for the use of  their servers  you can use tokens or coins  bits of code in order to transfer that  value in that system  in addition you can imagine a situation  where you could pay at a restaurant  with tokens you could imagine tokens  that are designed specifically for ride  sharing systems you could imagine and  construct tokens and coins that could be  used for just about any economic  function in addition to the store of  value  third we come to initial coin offerings  or initial token offerings  these are fascinating situations  imagine somebody who wants to build a  system  that allows you to use tokens in order  to make restaurant reservations you get  paid for making reviews  you can get premiums and discounts for  ordering special bottles of wine or what  have you  so they build these tokens or they plan  to build these tokens  and they tell people look  we will sell you tokens for a system  that doesn't yet exist  and we will sell you these tokens and we  will raise a certain amount of money  and when we raise that money we will  then use it to launch the system and you  will have the tokens that you can then  use in the system and people have been  raising lots and lots and lots of money  around these initial coin offerings  in 2018 alone through july 10 billion  dollars has been raised for these  initial coin offerings  as you might imagine these are a bit  controversial  if you stop and if you think about  initial coin offerings  in the real world  it's as though somebody says you know i  have an idea i'm going to come up with  something oh i don't know beanie babies  and instead of  selling you stock  in the company that's going to make  beanie babies  i'm going to sell you beanie babies  before i've actually made any beanie  babies in other words i'm pre-selling my  inventory  and i'm using the cash that i raise by  pre-selling the inventory in order to  build the company and build the  inventory that i'm going to sell to you  it's not selling stock  the people who buy it don't have any  voting rights  all they're doing as a practical matter  is  buying the future inventory but in most  situations  or virtually all situations  they're buying the inventory at a price  that they hope will appreciate  and it's in part because of that  expectation of appreciation that  expectation of a profit that we wind up  with a fascinating legal issue  as to whether these initial coin  offerings are actually sales of  securities that need to be regulated as  security sales by the united states  securities exchange commission  and whether they also give rise to  private rights of action for the failure  to register these sales and for fraud  that in my view commonly occurs in  conjunction with these sales
 

